       _c_u_r_s_e_s supports color attributes on  terminals  with  that  capability.
       Call  ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr  (typically  right  after iinniittssccrr(3X)) to enable this
       feature.  Colors are always used in pairs.   A  _c_o_l_o_r  _p_a_i_r  couples  a
       foreground  color  for characters with a background color for the blank
       field on which characters are rendered.  iinniitt__ppaaiirr initializes a  color
       pair.  The macro CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRR(_n) can then convert the pair to a video at‐
       tribute.

       If a terminal has the relevant capability, iinniitt__ccoolloorr permits (re)defi‐
       nition  of  a  color.   hhaass__ccoolloorrss  and ccaann__cchhaannggee__ccoolloorr return TTRRUUEE or
       FFAALLSSEE, depending on whether  the  terminal  has  color  capability  and
       whether  the  programmer  can change the colors.  ccoolloorr__ccoonntteenntt permits
       extraction of the red, green, and blue  components  of  an  initialized
       color.   ppaaiirr__ccoonntteenntt permits discovery of a color pair's current defi‐
       nition.

   RReennddeerriinngg
       _c_u_r_s_e_s combines the following data to render a character cell.  Any  of
       them can include color information.

       •   _c_u_r_s_e_s character attributes, as from wwaaddddcchh(3X) or wwaadddd__wwcchh(3X)

       •   window attributes, as from wwaattttrrsseett(3X) or wwaattttrr__sseett(3X)

       •   window  background  character  attributes,  as from wwbbkkggddsseett(3X) or
           wwbbkkggrrnnddsseett(3X)

       Per-character and window attributes are usually set through a  function
       parameter  containing  attributes  including  a color pair value.  Some
       functions, such as wwaattttrr__sseett, use a separate color pair number  parame‐
       ter.

       The  background  character  is  a special case: it includes a character
       code, just as if it were passed to wwaaddddcchh.

       The _c_u_r_s_e_s library does the actual work of combining these color  pairs
       in an internal function called from wwaaddddcchh:

       •   If the parameter passed to wwaaddddcchh is _b_l_a_n_k, and it uses the special
           color pair 0,

           •   _c_u_r_s_e_s next checks the window attribute.

           •   If the window attribute does not use color pair 0, _c_u_r_s_e_s  uses
               the color pair from the window attribute.

           •   Otherwise, _c_u_r_s_e_s uses the background character.

       •   If  the parameter passed to wwaaddddcchh is _n_o_t _b_l_a_n_k, or it does not use
           the special color pair 0, _c_u_r_s_e_s prefers the color  pair  from  the
           parameter, if it is nonzero.  Otherwise, it tries the window attri‐
           bute next, and finally the background character.

       Some _c_u_r_s_e_s functions such as wwpprriinnttww call wwaaddddcchh.  Those do  not  com‐
       bine  its  parameter  with  a color pair.  Consequently those calls use
       only the window attribute or the background character.

