   ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr
       The ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr routine requires no arguments.  It must  be  called  if
       the  programmer wants to use colors, and before any other color manipu‐
       lation routine is called.  It is good practice  to  call  this  routine
       right after iinniittssccrr.  ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr does this:

       •   It  initializes  two  global variables, CCOOLLOORRSS and CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRRSS (re‐
           spectively defining the maximum number of colors  and  color  pairs
           the terminal can support).

       •   It  initializes  the special color pair 00 to the default foreground
           and background colors.  No other color pairs are initialized.

       •   It restores the colors on the terminal to the values they had  when
           the terminal was just turned on.

       •   If  the  terminal supports the iinniittcc (iinniittiiaalliizzee__ccoolloorr) capability,
           ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr initializes its internal table  representing  the  red,
           green, and blue components of the color palette.

           The components depend on whether the terminal uses CGA (aka “ANSI”)
           or HLS (i.e.,  the  hhllss  (hhuuee__lliigghhttnneessss__ssaattuurraattiioonn)  capability  is
           set).   The  table  is  initialized  first  for  eight basic colors
           (black, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, and white),  using
           weights that depend upon the CGA/HLS choice.  For “ANSI” colors the
           weights are 668800 or 00 depending on whether  the  corresponding  red,
           green,  or  blue component is used or not.  That permits using 11000000
           to represent bold/bright colors.  After the  initial  eight  colors
           (if  the  terminal  supports more than eight colors) the components
           are initialized using the same pattern, but with weights  of  11000000.
           SVr4 uses a similar scheme, but uses 11000000 for the components of the
           initial eight colors.

           ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr does not attempt to set the terminal's color palette to
           match its built-in table.  An application may use iinniitt__ccoolloorr to al‐
           ter the internal table along with the terminal's color.

       These limits apply to color values and  color  pairs.   Values  outside
       these limits are not valid, and may result in a runtime error:

       •   CCOOLLOORRSS  corresponds  to the terminal database's mmaaxx__ccoolloorrss capabil‐
           ity, (see tteerrmmiinnffoo(5)).

       •   color values are expected to be in the range 00 to CCOOLLOORRSS--11,  inclu‐
           sive (including 00 and CCOOLLOORRSS--11).

       •   a  special  color value --11 is used in certain extended functions to
           denote the _d_e_f_a_u_l_t _c_o_l_o_r (see uussee__ddeeffaauulltt__ccoolloorrss(3X)).

       •   CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRRSS corresponds to the terminal database's mmaaxx__ppaaiirrss  capa‐
           bility, (see tteerrmmiinnffoo(5)).

       •   valid color pair values are in the range 11 to CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRRSS--11, inclu‐
           sive.

       •   color pair 00 is special; it denotes “no color”.

           Color pair 00 is assumed to be white on black, but is actually what‐
           ever  the terminal implements before color is initialized.  It can‐
           not be modified by the application.

   hhaass__ccoolloorrss
       The hhaass__ccoolloorrss routine requires no arguments.  It returns TTRRUUEE  if  the
       terminal can manipulate colors; otherwise, it returns FFAALLSSEE.  This rou‐
       tine facilitates writing terminal-independent programs.  For example, a
       programmer  can  use  it  to  decide whether to use color or some other
       video attribute.

   ccaann__cchhaannggee__ccoolloorr
       The ccaann__cchhaannggee__ccoolloorr routine requires no arguments.  It returns TTRRUUEE if
       the  terminal  supports colors and can change their definitions; other,
       it returns FFAALLSSEE.  This routine facilitates  writing  terminal-indepen‐
       dent programs.

   iinniitt__ppaaiirr
       The iinniitt__ppaaiirr routine changes the definition of a color pair.  It takes
       three arguments: the number of the color pair to be changed, the  fore‐
       ground color number, and the background color number.  For portable ap‐
       plications:

       •   The first argument must be a valid color pair  value.   If  default
           colors are used (see uussee__ddeeffaauulltt__ccoolloorrss(3X)) the upper limit is ad‐
           justed to allow for extra pairs which use a default color in  fore‐
           ground and/or background.

       •   The second and third arguments must be valid color values.

       If  the  color pair was previously initialized, the screen is refreshed
       and all occurrences of that color pair are changed to the  new  defini‐
       tion.

       As  an  extension,  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  allows  you  to  set  color pair 00 via the
       aassssuummee__ddeeffaauulltt__ccoolloorrss(3X) routine, or to specify  the  use  of  default
       colors (color number --11) if you first invoke the uussee__ddeeffaauulltt__ccoolloorrss(3X)
       routine.

   iinniitt__eexxtteennddeedd__ppaaiirr
       Because iinniitt__ppaaiirr uses signed sshhoorrtts for its  parameters,  that  limits
